.\" /usr/bin/rcon — the standalone client. `mc rcon` is mc-rcon(1).
.TH RCON 1 "" "mc" "mc manual"
.SH NAME
rcon \- standalone Minecraft RCON client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rcon
.BI \-\-password\-file " FILE"
.I HOST PORT
.RI [ command ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A minimal RCON client, installed by the
.B mc-rcon
package. It is useful by hand and by script, and it is what a member of the
.B minecraft
group can run without root.
.PP
With no
.IR command ,
it opens an interactive session and reads commands from standard input, one per
line, until end of file. With a
.IR command ,
it sends that one, prints the reply and exits. Everything after
.I PORT
is the command, including anything that starts with a dash.
.PP
For the server this host manages,
.BR "mc rcon" (1)
is the easier route: it resolves the port and the password file for you. Use
this when you need neither.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-password\-file " FILE"
Read the password from
.IR FILE ,
trimming trailing whitespace. Required.
.IP
There is deliberately no option to pass the password itself.
.I /proc/<pid>/cmdline
is world-readable, so a password in argv is visible to every local user on the
machine for as long as the process runs.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I HOST
Must resolve to a loopback address. The client refuses anything else \(em
including a name that resolves to both a loopback and a public address, since
which one a connection lands on is not the caller's choice. RCON is
unencrypted; it has no business on a network.
.TP
.I PORT
The RCON port, not the game port. Usually the game port plus 10.
.B mc rcon status
prints the one in use.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.nf
.RS
rcon \-\-password\-file /etc/minecraft/server.passwd 127.0.0.1 25575 list
.RE
.fi
.PP
Interactive, as a member of the
.B minecraft
group:
.PP
.nf
.RS
rcon \-\-password\-file /etc/minecraft/server.passwd 127.0.0.1 25575
.RE
.fi
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
The command was sent and a reply received.
.TP
.B 1
Bad usage, an unreadable password file, a refused host, a failed
authentication, or a connection that could not be made.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/minecraft/server.passwd
Where mc keeps the password for the server on this host.
.BR "root:minecraft 0640" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mc-rcon (1),
.BR mc (1)
